ng round, followed by Canada and China, with the USA in fourth, although the Aussies and Americans swam B teams, and would replace their entire squads for the final."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the final, " [:a {:href "/athletes/147282"} "Regan Smith"] " put the USA in first on the opening backstroke leg, with an Olympic record 52.28, ahead of " [:a {:href "/athletes/2504470"} "Kaylee McKeown"] ", who had beaten Smith in the 100 back in Paris, and the race was never close. The Americans extended their lead on the next two legs, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/132158"} "Lilly King"] "’s breaststroke putting them over 2½ seconds ahead and, although " [:a {:href "/athletes/140694"} "Mollie O’Callaghan"] " closed slightly on " [:a {:href "/athletes/147283"} "Torri Huske"] " on the anchor, the USA won in a world record 3:49.63, 3.48 seconds ahead of Australia. China had been second at the final exchange but " [:a {:href "/athletes/2504727"} "Yang Junxuan"] " could not match O’Callaghan on the anchor leg."] "\n\n" [:p {} "McKeown’s silver medal gave her 14 Olympic medals, tied for the most by any woman ever, equaling " [:a {:href "/athletes/126653"} "Katie Ledecky"] ", who also won her 14th medal in Paris. O’Callaghan’s won her eighth Olympic medal, seven of them coming in relays at Tokyo and Paris, along with 200 free gold in 2024."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "United States"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "3:56.40 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "3:49.63 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "10"} [:a {:href "/athletes/147282"} "Regan Smith"] " • " [:a {:href "